2. Map each mathematical concept to the corresponding Mathlib definition available in the given context (use the context's own definitions and local notations where provided).
3. State the theorem with exactly the hypotheses of the problem — no added assumptions (no positivity, coprimality, or nondegeneracy conditions the text never states), no narrowed domains — as named arguments, and the full conclusion as the target; inline any auxiliary definitions as lambdas (never `let`).
4. Verify consistency: mentally type-check the statement — syntax (especially `∈`, never `in`, in big-operator binders), binder names, scope, types, correct constant names (`‖·‖` instead of `Complex.abs`, `EReal` instead of `ℝ` whenever `⊤`/`⊥` appear), and explicit coercions where domains meet — and then mentally judge faithfulness (equivalent strength to the original problem: no extra hypotheses, no missing quantifiers, no weakened conclusion, side conditions covering all cases) before finalizing.
